  "problem": "A certain company produces two types of motorcycles: classic and high-cylinder. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of motorcycles each decade:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Classic** | **High-Cylinder** |\n| To: | **Classic** | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |\n| **High-Cylinder** | $0.4$ | $0.8$ |  |\n\nThat is, $40%$ of classic motorcycle customers switch to high-cylinder each decade, and $20%$ of high-cylinder motorcycle customers switch to Classic.\n\nCurrently, $7\\,000$ customers use classic motorcycles while $3\\,000$ customers use high-cylinder motorcycles. How many customers will use high-cylinder motorcycles in one decade? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change.",
